tension: keeping it fixed ensures honest measurement, but over many loop iterations it becomes saturated and overfit to the current model's behavior.\n\nHuman-authored training data needs explicit quality criteria too—not every session should become a training example, but there's no requirement defining which ones are \"good enough.\" And there's a concurrency gap: while the system mentions agents picking up board tasks, there's no functional requirement for mutual exclusion or a claim protocol to prevent two machines from training simultaneously and corrupting the model.\n\nClock skew across machines is another open question that probably deserves to be a hard requirement rather than left as a design choice. Then there's the resource side—the system records cost but has no budget ceiling or guards against blowing the compute/thermal budget on the T480+eGPU setup, which can wedge the hardware. And finally, loop liveness itself: if an agent dies mid-turn, the system needs explicit alerting or a dead-man's switch rather than just relying on observability to catch it after the fact.\n\nThere's also a security gap around agent autonomy—these loops run unsupervised modifying training data and models, but there's no inbound integrity check on staged raw sessions coming from many machines. A compromised session could poison the training corpus. On the metrics side, \"better\" in R23 is vague: if eval improves but cost regresses, which wins? Need a clear requirement for how to aggregate across multiple objectives.\n\nThe user's second ask is about using the local agent to do most of the delivery work, then feeding those sessions back as training data for the house model—essentially dogfooding the self-learning system itself. I should note this is meta and add a requirement that the delivery of this system uses the loop.\n\nBut I need to flag the model-collapse risk: if we're training on frontier-generated sessions, we need provenance guards to avoid degradation. The practical deliverable here isn't to actually orchestrate the local agent right now—this is a requirements review—so I'll focus on giving a verdict on the doc, then concrete gaps organized as priorities, each framed clearly.\n\nI'll present the gap analysis in-chat and offer to add them as numbered items to the doc rather than asking permission first. Given the owner's preference for board-level discipline and direct feedback, crisp numbered gaps will land better than a question.\n\nThe strongest gaps to flag are model collapse without provenance guards, the lack of human-signed gates on model promotion, single-point-of-failure risk on the portable drive, unbounded storage growth, and no inbound validation on sessions from multiple machines.\n\nBeyond those, I need to surface exactly-once guarantees across machines to prevent trainer corruption, the tension between freezing eval sets for comparability versus evolving them, how to make promotion decisions when metrics trade off against each other, handling semantic conflicts that bi-temporal ordering alone can't resolve, managing schema evolution through historical replay, and ensuring loop liveness with a dead-man switch.\n\nI'll organize these twelve issues into tiers marking which are critical versus nice-to-have, then move into the local-agent strategy and write them into the doc as new requirements.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14067,"cache_read_input_tokens":43995,"output_tokens":5831,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14067,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5831,"cache_read_input_tokens":43995,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14067,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14067},"type":"message"}],"