{"parentUuid":"2cc50e70-92c8-4a31-9862-3c06643ef396","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-8","id":"msg_011CdNoKR11CqMZw9jZHSSuv","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read it end to end, plus your CLAUDE.md and the memory index for context. This is a strong, disciplined requirements doc — the bi-temporal / additive / delta spine is coherent, and it correctly defers design. It reads as genuinely complete on the *substrate* (waves 1–2). The gaps are almost all in the **loop** (wave 3) — which is exactly the part that turns a data pipeline into a \"self-learning system,\" and therefore the part where the sharp risks live.\n\n## Gaps worth adding (numbered so they slot into your R# scheme)\n\n**Tier 1 — must-add; these are correctness/safety holes, not polish**\n\n- **R38 — Self-training contamination / model-collapse guard.** This is the biggest one and it's structural. The flywheel feeds *sessions → synthesis → training → model → sessions*. As the local model authors more sessions, you get a distillation-of-self loop that drifts and collapses. Nothing in R22–R30 requires tracking **authorship provenance of the training signal** (human vs frontier-model vs house-model) or capping the house-model's own output as a fraction of any training turn. Without it, \"self-learning\" can silently become \"self-poisoning.\" This one directly bears on your second ask below.\n- **R39 — Human-signed model promotion.** Your doctrine is *human-signed stops* ([[owner-working-style]]), but R22 says hand-offs are \"automatable and gated\" without saying the serving-promotion gate requires a signature. A loop that auto-swaps model N→N+1 behind the router violates that doctrine. Make promotion-to-served an explicit human-signed gate, and require **rollback/pinning** of the served model (which N+1 replaced, how to revert). R36 additivity implies it but doesn't state it.\n- **R40 — Input integrity / poison gate on staged raw.** The leak gate (R31) is *outbound* privacy only. Nothing validates *inbound* sessions arriving from many machines. A prompt-injected or corrupted session on one machine can flow straight into synthesis→training. You need a trust boundary / validation on staged raw, symmetric to the leak gate.\n- **R41 — Durability of the portable drive.** R17 makes **D:** the *single point of truth* and R21 keeps it local-first — but append-only ≠ backed up. One drive = one point of failure for the entire corpus. Require a backup/replication + integrity-check (checksums, restore drill) requirement. This is a real, mundane way to lose everything.\n\n**Tier 2 — should-add; real but lower-severity**\n\n- **R42 — Storage growth / compaction budget.** Append-only + bi-temporal + never-delete on a finite portable drive → unbounded growth with no stated ceiling, archival, or compaction policy. Additive doctrine and a full drive will collide.\n- **R43 — Exactly-once cross-machine work claiming.** Currently only Open Q5. But two machines training/merging concurrently can corrupt the substrate — that's a hard requirement, not a design nicety. Promote the \"exactly-one-winner claim race\" to an R#.\n- **R44 — Promotion decision function under multiple metrics.** R23's \"model N+1 ≥ model N\" is undefined when eval improves but cost/latency regresses (a Pareto case). Define the decision function / dominance rule, or you'll relitigate \"is this better?\" every turn.\n- **R45 — Eval-set evolution vs. frozen comparability.** R23/R24 freeze the rubric for honesty — correct — but a frozen instrument saturates as capability grows, and cross-turn comparability breaks the moment you extend it. Require an *additive* eval-growth protocol (new held-out slices added without invalidating the historical series).\n\n**Tier 3 — flag in Open Questions rather than as R#**\n\n- **Semantic conflict resolution.** R11 resolves *ordering* via valid-time, but not two machines deriving *contradictory* facts at the same valid-time. Bi-temporal doesn't adjudicate truth.\n- **Schema evolution across bi-temporal history.** R32 requires replay; evolving the card/graph/dataset schema over months makes old records un-replayable unless migration is versioned. Add to Open Q8 (backfill) or its own.\n- **Loop-level liveness / dead-man switch.** R35 gives observability-by-default, but your own hard-won lesson is *\"a tqdm line ≠ alive\"* ([[verify-gpu-job-liveness]]). Add a loop-level liveness requirement (a stalled agent loop must be *detected*, not just logged).\n- **Resource/thermal ceiling + abort.** The eGPU wedges transiently ([[windows-rocm-training-gotchas]]); an unattended loop needs a resource/thermal guard that aborts cleanly rather than wedging the box mid-turn.\n\n## On \"use the local agent as much as possible to deliver this, then train the house agent from these sessions\"\n\nThis is the right instinct and it's **dogfooding the flywheel to build the flywheel** — but note the sharp edge it creates, which is precisely **R38 above**: if the house agent both *does* the delivery work and *learns from* the sessions doing it, you're on the self-training loop from turn one. That's fine and desirable **only if** provenance is tracked and frontier-authored corrections stay in the mix. So the guard isn't optional overhead — it's the thing that makes your requested workflow safe.\n\nConcretely, how I'd run it (fits your existing board + skills, no new machinery):\n\n1. **Split by altitude, per your `author≠verifier` rule.** Let the **house model** (LM Studio on the eGPU, via `house-trainer` / `longview-pipeline`) do the high-volume, well-specified work — synthesis phases, dataset builds, delta runs. Reserve frontier sessions (this one) for **requirements, design, gate-writing, and verification** — the judgment the house model can't yet do reliably.\n2. **Capture every session as staged raw *with authorship tags* (R38).** Each session records whether it was house- or frontier-authored, and which parts were frontier *corrections* of house output. Those corrections are the highest-value training signal — they're exactly method-in-weights teaching (R25).\n3. **Frontier-verified only into training.** A house-authored session becomes a *training* row only after it passes a verifier gate (frozen rubric or frontier sign-off), so you train on *validated* method, not raw self-output. This is R38 + R44 doing their job.\n4. **Measure the handoff itself.** Track the fraction of delivery work the house model carries per loop turn as an explicit optimization target (R30) — that number going up *while* held-out eval holds or rises is the real proof the flywheel compounds, not just that the corpus grew (R26).\n\nOne practical note: the plugin MCP servers listed for this session (github, linear, slack, etc.) all need auth and this session is non-interactive, so I can't drive them here — not that this task needs them. The delivery you're describing runs entirely on your local stack (LM Studio, house-trainer, the board), which is the point.\n\n---\n\nWant me to **append these as R38–R45 + three new Open Questions** directly into the doc (marked as a reviewer addendum so the author≠reviewer line stays clean), or keep them here for you to fold in yourself? I'd recommend the former — I'll add them as a clearly-marked `## 9. Reviewer addendum (gaps)` section so nothing above it is mutated, consistent with your additive doctrine."}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14067,"cache_read_input_tokens":43995,"output_tokens":5831,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14067,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5831,"cache_read_input_tokens":43995,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14067,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemera